      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 American Association for Clinical Chemistry.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Obesity has risen dramatically in recent decades in the US and most other countries of the world. This has led to a search for public policies and other interventions that can prevent obesity and improve diets. However, there remain considerable uncertainty and confusion about the effectiveness for many potential policies."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "CONTENT",
            "content": "This review assesses the strength of the research evidence for 4 commonly proposed policies to prevent obesity and improve diets: (a) taxes on sugar-sweetened beverages (SSBs); (b) calorie labels on restaurant menus; (c) restricting food advertising to youth; and (d) excluding energy-dense foods from the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "SUMMARY",
            "content": "The existing literature has many limitations. Often, the research designs are weak, with small nonrepresentative samples and only short-run follow-up. On the basis of the available evidence, there appears to be no magic bullet to prevent and reduce obesity. Thus, a suite of these policies may be needed for a meaningful impact."
          }
        ]
      },
